{
  "requirement": "A definition glob MUST match a complete full name. `*` MUST match zero or more characters, including dots; all other valid name characters MUST match literally. An empty glob MUST be invalid. Every definition selection MUST use this matching rule, including requirements evidence inputs, CLI definition filters, and the authoring `references` tool, and MUST match against both requirements and named examples.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "Obligations and the tests that check them:\n\n- **Whole-name match.** `src/definition_glob.rs#mod[tests]/fn[wildcard_crosses_dot_segments_and_matches_the_whole_name]` rejects both a prefix and a suffix: `foo.*.bar` doesn't match `prefix.foo.x.bar` or `foo.x.bar.suffix`.\n- **`*` matches zero or more characters, including dots.** The same test shows `foo.*.bar` matching `foo.x.bar` and `foo.x.y.bar`. `foo.bar.*` doesn't match `foo.bar`, so the literal dot before `*` is still required. The zero-character case is covered by `tests/commands.rs#fn[commands_report_invalid_arguments_and_unmatched_selectors]`, where `status requirements.*ready` selects both `requirements.ready` and `requirements.xready`. That test also asserts that `requirements.pending` is excluded.\n- **Other characters match literally.** The unit test shows that `.` is not a wildcard (`foo.bar` vs `fooxbar`). It also checks `_` and `-` against near-miss names.\n- **Empty glob invalid.** The unit test asserts `validate(\"\")` is an error. `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order]` checks that an empty requirements-input glob is reported as invalid with \"definition glob must not be empty\".\n- **Invalid syntax.** The unit test rejects `foo[bar]`. The evidence test rejects `foo.[a].bar`. The CLI test checks that `info [` and `status [` fail with \"invalid definition glob\". The authoring lookup test checks that `[bad]` returns an \"invalid definition glob\" error.\n- **Every selection uses this rule.**\n  - Evidence inputs: the evidence test covers cross-dot matching, input change and added/removed matches, and an unmatched glob. `tests/definitions.rs#fn[selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions]` covers `rules.*`.\n  - CLI filters: the commands test covers `status` and `info`, and the definitions test runs `status rules.*` and `info rules.*`.\n  - Authoring tool: `src/authoring/domain_tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[domain_discovery_returns_readable_nodes_without_recording_their_contents]` checks `requirements.s*` selecting a subset, plus the invalid-glob error. The lookup tool and the other surfaces all call the shared `validate` and `matches` functions.\n- **Both requirements and named examples.** The evidence test matches an example `a.b.bar` and a requirement `a.bar` through `foo.*.bar`. The definitions test matches `rules.*` against a requirement and an example, and shows that a definition with the same name is not selected. The lookup test matches an example by glob.\n\nThe remaining untested combinations are an empty glob through the CLI and the lookup tool, and a requirement-only glob through the lookup tool. They go through the same single `validate` and `matches` functions, which the unit and evidence tests cover. I don't count them as gaps.",
